  "summary": "A traditional Japanese paper technique where ink bleeds slightly along the drawn line, creating soft, organic edges.",
  "description": "Hitokoshi involves using washi paper with specific absorbency properties that cause controlled ink bleeding, resulting in lines with soft, feathery edges rather than crisp boundaries. This technique developed in Japan during the Edo Period for ukiyo-e prints and book illustrations, where paper makers and printers collaborated to create effects that enhanced the organic quality of printed images.",
